# "Your" for "you're" before words that only follow "you are". Common-error
# lists; examples are ours.
# why: You're means "you are" (you're invited). Your means "belonging to you" (your invitation). If you can say "you are", write you're.
expr main [<(your [able, kidding, done, not, probably, definitely]), your>, <(your [going, invited, supposed, allowed, expected, required, welcome] [to, for]), your>]

let message "Did you mean `you're` (you are)?"
let description "Fixes `your invited` → `you're invited`."
let kind "Grammar"
let becomes "you're"
let strategy "MatchCase"

test "The mayor said that your invited to the opening." "The mayor said that you're invited to the opening."
test "Your able to change it in the settings." "You're able to change it in the settings."
test "I think your going to like it." "I think you're going to like it."
test "Your not alone." "You're not alone."

allows "You're invited to the opening."
allows "Your invitation is attached."
allows "Thanks for your help."
allows "Your right to appeal is explained below."
allows "Bring your invited guests to the front."
allows "Your finished product will ship Monday."
allows "It was your very own idea."
allows "I can't wait for your going-away party."
